<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526)</h1><p class="st-meta">1102 CE – 1526 CE · capital Knin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Croatian kingdom in personal union with Hungary, governed by bans and a noble diet, until the disaster of Mohács led it to the Habsburgs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trpimirovic-dynasty">Trpimirović</a> line ended and the last king fell at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gvozd-mountain">Gvozd</a> in 1097, Coloman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biograd-na-moru">Biograd</a> in 1102. Later tradition held that a pact (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacta-conventa-croatia">Pacta conventa</a>) with Croatian nobles guaranteed their rights, though its authenticity is disputed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom kept its own institutions: a ban governed for the king, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Sabor</a> (diet) of nobles met separately. Great families such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subic-family">Šubići of Bribir</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankopan-family">Frankopans</a> held vast lands; Ban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-i-subic-of-bribir">Paul I Šubić</a> was almost independent around 1300. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> fought over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> for centuries, and in 1409 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a> sold his Dalmatian claims to Venice. Ottoman raids grew after 1463, with a heavy defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-krbava-field">Krbava Field</a> in 1493.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-hungary">Louis II</a> died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Mohács</a> in 1526, the Croatian nobles meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1527-election-in-cetin">Cetin</a> in 1527 elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand of Habsburg</a> as king, beginning centuries of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526)" width="320" height="272"><figcaption>Image: Tezwoo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Croatia_1260.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1102 CE – 1526 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Knin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Coloman, Paul I Šubić, Louis I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Croatian; Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Croatia in union with Hungary, Kingdom of Croatia and Dalmatia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1314&amp;focus=kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526" aria-label="Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5068 618 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5388 874L5370 878M5388 874L5446 783M5388 874L5446 783M5388 874L5378 819"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5370 878h0M5446 783h0M5446 783h0M5378 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5388 874h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5388 874h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Knin. <a href="/atlas?year=1314&amp;fo